# Google Search Console: What Every Website Owner Should Know

You have a website. People can visit it, your pages are live, and your business is finally online.

But how do you know whether Google can find your website?

How many people discover it through Google Search? Which search terms bring visitors? Are some of your pages missing from Google's index? Are there technical problems affecting your website?

You could spend hours guessing, but there's a better way to find out.

It's called **Google Search Console**.

Google Search Console is one of the most useful free tools available to website owners. It helps you understand how your website performs in Google Search, monitor indexing, identify certain technical issues, and discover opportunities to improve your online visibility.

Whether you run a small business website, a personal portfolio, an online store, or a blog, understanding this tool can make managing your website much easier.

Let's explore what you need to know.

## 1. What Is Google Search Console?

Google Search Console, commonly called GSC, is a free service from Google that helps website owners monitor and understand their website's presence in Google Search.

It provides information about how Google discovers, crawls, and indexes your website, alongside reports about how your pages perform in search results.

With Google Search Console, you can:

- See which search queries bring impressions and clicks.
- Check whether important pages are indexed.
- Submit your XML sitemap.
- Inspect individual website URLs.
- Identify certain crawling and indexing problems.
- Monitor your website's search performance.
- Review links Google has discovered.
- Check for certain security issues and manual actions.

Think of it as a monitoring dashboard for your website's relationship with Google Search.

It doesn't replace your website analytics tool, but it gives you information that analytics platforms may not provide in the same way.

## 2. Why Does Every Website Owner Need Google Search Console?

You don't necessarily need to become an SEO expert to manage your website.

However, you should understand whether your website is discoverable and how it performs in search results.

Here are some reasons Google Search Console is useful.

### A. Know Whether Google Is Finding Your Website

Imagine launching a new business website and assuming everything is fine because the homepage loads correctly.

But what if Google hasn't discovered some of your important pages?

Search Console helps you investigate whether Google has crawled and indexed your URLs.

You can identify pages that are indexed, pages that aren't indexed, and some of the reasons Google reports for excluding particular URLs.

### B. Understand How People Find Your Website

Search Console shows you the queries people use when your website appears in Google Search.

For example, a photography website might receive impressions for searches such as:

- Wedding photographer in Lagos
- Portrait photography services
- Professional event photographer
- Photography packages

This information helps you understand what people are searching for when they encounter your website.

You may even discover search terms you weren't intentionally targeting.

### C. Identify Problems Before They Become Bigger Issues

Search Console can alert you to certain problems affecting your website's search presence.

For example:

- Important pages are not indexed.
- Google encounters crawling errors.
- Some pages have mobile usability or page experience concerns.
- Your website has security issues.
- Google has applied a manual action.

It doesn't detect every possible website problem, but it gives you a useful place to investigate search-related issues.

### D. Make Better SEO Decisions

Instead of guessing which pages need improvement, you can use actual search performance data.

You might discover that:

- One article receives many impressions but few clicks.
- A service page appears for relevant searches but has a low average position.
- A particular page attracts more search traffic than expected.
- Some search queries reveal opportunities for new content.

These insights can guide your next SEO improvements.

## 3. How to Set Up Google Search Console

Getting started is relatively straightforward.

You need access to your website and a Google account.

### Step 1: Open Google Search Console

Visit:

[https://search.google.com/search-console/](https://search.google.com/search-console/)

Sign in using your Google account.

### Step 2: Add Your Website

Google will ask you to choose a property type.

You have two main options:

**Domain Property**

This covers all subdomains and URL variations associated with your domain.

For example, it can cover:

- `https://example.com`
- `https://www.example.com`
- `http://example.com`
- `https://blog.example.com`

Domain properties require DNS verification.

**URL Prefix Property**

This covers only the exact URL prefix you enter.

For example, adding:

`https://example.com/`

does not automatically cover:

`https://www.example.com/`

or:

`http://example.com/`

URL-prefix properties support several verification methods, including HTML file upload, HTML tag, Google Analytics, and Google Tag Manager, depending on your setup.

**Which should you choose?**

For most website owners, a Domain property is a good option because it provides a broader view of the domain.

However, a URL-prefix property can be convenient if you only need to monitor a particular website version or have a verification method already configured.

### Step 3: Verify Website Ownership

Google needs to confirm that you own or have permission to manage the website.

For Domain properties, this usually involves adding a DNS TXT record through your domain provider.

For URL-prefix properties, available methods may include:

- Uploading an HTML verification file.
- Adding an HTML meta tag.
- Using Google Analytics.
- Using Google Tag Manager.
- DNS verification.

Follow the instructions shown in Search Console for your selected method.

Once verification is successful, your property is ready.

### Step 4: Submit Your Sitemap

After setting up Search Console, submit your website's XML sitemap.

A common sitemap address is:

`https://example.com/sitemap.xml`

However, your website may use a different sitemap location.

To submit it:

1. Open your Search Console property.
1. Select **Sitemaps** from the navigation.
1. Enter the sitemap URL.
1. Click Submit.

Google will attempt to read the sitemap and use it to discover your pages.

Remember, submitting a sitemap does not guarantee that every URL will be indexed.

For a more detailed explanation of getting your website discovered, read our guide on [How to Get Your New Website on Google](https://emmystack01.com/articles/how-to-get-your-new-website-on-google).

## 4. Understanding the Important Google Search Console Reports

Once your website is connected, you'll find different reports and tools in the Search Console dashboard.

You don't need to understand everything immediately.

Start with these important sections.

### A. Performance Report

This is one of the most useful sections for understanding how people discover your website through Google Search.

It shows several important metrics.

**Total Clicks**

The number of times people clicked your website's result in Google Search during the selected period.

For example, if someone searches for a service, sees your website, and clicks it, that contributes to your total clicks.

**Total Impressions**

The number of times a link to your website appeared in Google's search results.

An impression doesn't necessarily mean someone noticed or interacted with your listing. It means Google recorded it as being shown.

**Average CTR**

CTR means Click-Through Rate.

It represents the percentage of recorded impressions that resulted in clicks.

The calculation is:

`CTR = (Clicks ÷ Impressions) × 100`

For example, if your website receives 1,000 impressions and 50 clicks:

`(50 ÷ 1,000) × 100 = 5%`

Your average CTR is 5%.

**Average Position**

This gives an aggregated indication of where your website's highest-positioned result appeared for the recorded impressions.

For example, an average position of 5 means your highest-positioned result appeared around fifth on average across the relevant impressions.

It doesn't mean every page of your website ranks fifth.

Search results can vary depending on the query, location, device, and other factors.

### What Can You Learn from the Performance Report?

Let's imagine your website receives the following results:

{% table %}
- Search Query
- Impressions
- Clicks
---
- Website designer in Lagos
- 1,200
- 60
---
- Affordable business website
- 800
- 24
---
- Professional website design
- 2,000
- 30
{% /table %}

What does this tell you?

Your website appeared for all three searches, but the number of clicks differs.

You could investigate why some queries receive more clicks than others.

Perhaps your page title needs improvement. Maybe the content doesn't clearly communicate what visitors will find. Or perhaps the search terms have different levels of competition and intent.

The important thing is that you now have information to investigate instead of relying entirely on assumptions.

You can also filter the report by:

- Queries
- Pages
- Countries
- Devices
- Search appearance
- Date ranges

This makes it easier to understand where your search traffic comes from.

### B. URL Inspection Tool

The URL Inspection tool helps you investigate individual pages on your website.

You can enter a URL and see information about Google's understanding of that page.

It can help you determine:

- Whether the URL is indexed.
- Whether Google has successfully crawled it.
- The last crawl date, where available.
- Whether indexing is allowed.
- Which canonical URL Google selected.
- Whether certain indexing issues were detected.

You can also run a live test to check the page's current accessibility to Google.

If an eligible page isn't indexed, you may be able to select **Request Indexing**.

For example, imagine publishing a new article.

You can inspect its URL to check whether Google has indexed it.

If it hasn't, you can investigate the reported reason and request indexing where appropriate.

Remember that requesting indexing does not guarantee that Google will index the page or do so immediately.

### C. Page Indexing Report

The Page Indexing report helps you understand which pages Google has indexed and which pages it hasn't.

This is particularly useful when your website contains multiple pages.

You may find categories such as:

- Indexed pages.
- Pages excluded by noindex.
- Pages with redirects.
- Duplicate pages.
- Pages blocked by robots.txt.
- Pages with crawling or server-related issues.
- Pages discovered but not currently indexed.

Not every excluded URL is a problem.

For example, if you intentionally redirect an old page to a new one, the old URL generally doesn't need to appear in Google's index.

The important thing is to investigate whether your valuable pages are being excluded for reasons you didn't intend.

### D. Sitemaps Report

This report helps you monitor the XML sitemaps you've submitted to Google.

You can check:

- Whether Google successfully processed a sitemap.
- When it was last read.
- How many URLs Google discovered through it.
- Whether Google encountered sitemap-related errors.

If your sitemap has errors, investigate them and correct the file before resubmitting it.

Your sitemap should contain the canonical URLs of important pages you want Google to discover.

### E. Core Web Vitals Report

Core Web Vitals are Google's metrics for evaluating aspects of the user experience, particularly loading performance, interactivity, and visual stability.

The three main metrics are:

**Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)**

Measures how quickly the main content of a page becomes visible.

**Interaction to Next Paint (INP)**

Measures how responsive a page is to user interactions.

**Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)**

Measures unexpected movement of page elements while the page loads.

Search Console can show groups of URLs with good, poor, or needs-improvement assessments where sufficient data is available.

These reports use real-world user experience data, so a page may not have enough data to receive an assessment.

Improving these metrics can contribute to a better user experience, but perfect Core Web Vitals scores do not guarantee higher Google rankings.

### F. Manual Actions Report

Google has spam policies that websites are expected to follow.

If Google's reviewers determine that a website violates certain spam policies, it may receive a manual action.

A manual action can affect how some or all of the website appears in Google Search.

The Manual Actions report helps website owners check whether Google has applied such an action.

If you receive one, review the explanation, correct the underlying problem, and follow Google's reconsideration process where applicable.

Not every ranking drop or indexing problem is caused by a manual action.

### G. Security Issues Report

This section helps you identify certain security problems Google has detected on your website.

For example:

- Hacked content.
- Malware.
- Harmful downloads.
- Other security-related threats.

If Google detects a security issue, take it seriously.

A compromised website can put visitors at risk and affect its appearance in search results.

Investigate the issue, secure your website, remove harmful content, and follow Google's instructions for requesting a review when the problem has been resolved.

### H. Links Report

The Links report provides information about links Google has found pointing to and within your website.

It includes information about:

**External Links**

Links from other websites pointing to your pages.

**Internal Links**

Links connecting pages within your own website.

You can use this information to understand which pages receive links and how your website's internal structure is organised.

However, this isn't a complete record of every link on the internet.

Don't treat the number of links shown in this report as a perfect measurement of your website's authority.

## 5. How Often Should You Check Google Search Console?

You don't need to spend the entire day refreshing Search Console.

For most small business websites, checking it regularly is enough.

Here's a simple routine.

**After launching a new website:**

- Verify your website.
- Submit your sitemap.
- Inspect your homepage.
- Check the indexing status of important pages.

**When publishing new content:**

- Make sure the page is accessible.
- Check that it has appropriate internal links.
- Inspect the URL if you need to investigate its indexing status.

**Weekly:**

- Review important indexing issues.
- Check for security warnings or manual actions.
- Look for unusual changes in search performance.

**Monthly:**

- Review your top-performing pages.
- Identify queries generating impressions but few clicks.
- Check which pages are gaining or losing visibility.
- Look for new content opportunities.
- Review your website's overall search performance.

You can adjust this routine depending on your website's size and how frequently you publish or update content.

## 6. Google Search Console vs Google Analytics: What's the Difference?

This is a common source of confusion.

Both tools provide valuable information about your website, but they answer different questions.

{% table %}
- Feature
- Google Search Console
- Google Analytics 4
---
- Main purpose
- Understand Google Search performance
- Understand website and app usage
---
- Search impressions
- Yes
- Not as a primary report
---
- Search queries
- Yes, for Google Search data available to the property
- Limited search query information
---
- Search clicks
- Yes
- Visits can be measured through traffic acquisition reports
---
- Indexing information
- Yes
- No
---
- Crawling information
- Yes, through relevant reports and tools
- No
---
- Visitor behaviour
- Limited
- Yes
---
- Events and conversions
- Not its primary purpose
- Yes
---
- Traffic sources
- Google Search performance
- Multiple traffic sources
{% /table %}

Think of it this way:

**Google Search Console helps you understand how people discover your website through Google Search.**

**Google Analytics helps you understand what people do after they arrive on your website.**

For a more complete understanding of your website's performance, using both tools together is useful.

## 7. Common Google Search Console Mistakes to Avoid

Having Search Console connected doesn't automatically mean you're using it correctly.

Here are some mistakes to watch out for.

### Mistake 1: Assuming Every Excluded Page Is a Problem

Some pages shouldn't be indexed.

For example, duplicate URLs, redirected pages, and pages intentionally marked as noindex may be correctly excluded.

Investigate the reason before making changes.

### Mistake 2: Thinking Impressions Mean Website Visitors

An impression means your website appeared in a Google Search result.

It doesn't mean someone clicked or visited your website.

Use clicks when evaluating how many visits came from Google Search results.

### Mistake 3: Obsessing Over Average Position

Average position is useful, but it doesn't tell the entire story.

A page can have different positions for different queries, devices, and locations.

Look at the queries and pages behind the average rather than focusing on one number.

### Mistake 4: Repeatedly Requesting Indexing

Requesting indexing over and over doesn't force Google to crawl your website faster.

If a page isn't being indexed, investigate the reason instead.

### Mistake 5: Ignoring the Data

Search Console is most useful when you act on what you learn.

If certain pages receive impressions but very few clicks, investigate their titles, descriptions, relevance, and search intent.

If important pages aren't indexed, investigate the technical issues.

Don't just collect data. Use it to make improvements.

## 8. Does Google Search Console Improve Your Website's Rankings?

No, connecting Google Search Console does not directly improve your website's rankings.

It is a monitoring and diagnostic tool, not a ranking booster.

However, the information it provides can help you make decisions that support your SEO efforts.

For example:

- You discover that important pages aren't indexed and fix the underlying problems.
- You identify relevant search queries and create better content.
- You find pages with low click-through rates and improve their search listings.
- You identify technical issues that may affect crawling or user experience.

The improvements you make can help your website perform better over time.

Search Console simply helps you understand what may need attention.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### 1. Is Google Search Console free?

Yes. Google Search Console is free to use.

You only need a Google account and verified ownership or access to the website property.

### 2. Can I use Google Search Console without a website?

You need a website or an eligible website property that you can verify to use Search Console for monitoring its search performance.

It isn't a general keyword research tool for monitoring websites you don't own.

### 3. How long does Google Search Console take to show data?

It depends on the report.

Search performance data is not necessarily available immediately. It can take a few days to appear.

Indexing reports and other information may also take time to update after changes are made.

### 4. Does Google Search Console show all the keywords people search for?

No. It shows search queries associated with your website that Google makes available in its performance reports.

Some queries may be omitted for privacy or other reasons.

Therefore, the report should not be treated as a complete list of every search term that brings visitors to your website.

### 5. Can Google Search Console tell me why my website isn't ranking?

It can help you investigate certain issues, such as indexing problems, manual actions, and search performance.

However, it doesn't provide a complete explanation of Google's ranking decisions.

A website may be indexed and technically accessible but still perform poorly because its content isn't sufficiently relevant, useful, or competitive for particular searches.

### 6. Do I need a developer to set up Google Search Console?

Not necessarily.

Many website owners can set it up themselves, particularly if they have access to their domain's DNS settings or website administration.

However, you may need a developer or hosting provider to help with DNS verification, HTML file uploads, or technical issues.

## Final Thoughts

Your website shouldn't be something you launch and forget.

You need to understand whether people can discover it, how it performs in search results, and whether anything is preventing important pages from appearing on Google.

Google Search Console gives you a useful starting point.

You can monitor search performance, investigate indexing issues, discover what people are searching for, and identify areas where your website needs attention.

You don't have to understand every report immediately.

Start with the Performance report, URL Inspection, Page Indexing, and Sitemaps. As you become more comfortable, explore the other sections.

The important thing is to make informed improvements instead of guessing.

